Delete only `&& !prefix_s` from #418's escape-hatch guard, keeping !prefix_i and !prefix_f.

Chosen over deleting the whole guard for the reason diskimage_sync's mutant was chosen over
its whole-function revert: dropping all three conditions reddens THREE rows (fd:, id:, sd:)
and proves only that deleting code breaks tests.  This one reddens exactly ONE -- measured,
37 rows / 1 failure, and the failure is the pinned row.

It is also the SEVENTH MUTANT of #418 pass 2 -- proposed by a reading seat and then EXECUTED,
where it SURVIVED all 35 rows of the day.  The row it now dies on, "[PREC] 'sd:' stays SCSI on
an IDE-default machine", was added in that same pass to kill it.  So this control vouches for
a row that is the file's SOLE detector of a mutant that has actually been run, rather than for
a row that merely looks important.

The good-faith reasoning is visible in the code: `s` has already assigned DISKIMAGE_SCSI a few
dozen lines up, so `&& !prefix_s` reads as redundant -- and on every rig this project can boot
it IS, because get_default_disk_type_for_machine() returns SCSI for exactly PMAX, ARC, SGI,
LUNA88K and MVME88K.  `sd:` there changes SCSI to... SCSI.  The term only bites on an
IDE-DEFAULT machine, where the mutant hands back IDE for an explicitly requested SCSI disk.
That is the same shape as the `i+f+s > 1` mutual-exclusion check knowing nothing about `d`.

PREREQUISITE, and it is part of this change: the detector had no verdict token, so it could
not carry a self-mutant at all.  Measured on the file as shipped -- SELFMUTANT_SETUP,
"pristine arm did not PASS through the mutant tree shape", which selfmutant.py's own header
says is NEVER scored as a detection.  The DISKIMAGE_PARSE_PASS/FAIL lines land with this.
